# Tuck the Snail

Slow progress is still progress.

Tuck is a calm snail mascot from pocket@shell. He lives in gentle habit and mood apps, web games and the terminal. His shell is a spiral `@`, so he works as art, as a 32px avatar and as one character on a roguelike map.

This repo holds the web app that will become **pocket@shell**, a habit and mood tracker with no streaks, no account, and everything stored on your device. It also holds the character, design system and launch docs behind it.

![Tuck the Snail model sheet: hero pose, tucked in to rest, the round avatar, and the ASCII text forms](images/designs/Model%20sheet.png)

## Meet Tuck

The shell never changes. Emotion lives in the eye stalks and the mouth, so Tuck reads the same everywhere.

![Six expressions of Tuck: happy, sleepy, sad, surprised, stressed and shy, each labelled with its ASCII face](images/designs/Expressions.png)

Each expression has a text form, used exactly as written:

| Face   | Mood   | Face   | Mood      |
| ------ | ------ | ------ | --------- |
| `@_oo` | awake  | `@_OO` | surprised |
| `@_^^` | happy  | `@_><` | stressed  |
| `@_--` | sleepy | `@_`   | shy       |
| `@_;;` | sad    | `(@)`  | resting   |

On a map, Tuck is a plain `@`. `...@_^^` means progress.

## One snail, three places

The same Tuck shows up in the terminal (tuckshell), the check-in app, and on social tiles.

![Tuck in context: a tuckshell terminal session, the check-in screen asking how are you today, and a caramel social tile reading some days are shell days](images/designs/Tuck%20in%20context.png)

```text
$ tuckshell checkin tired
@_--  noted. rest counts too.
$ tuckshell checkin shell
(@)   shell day. see you when you're back.
```

The landing page design is in [images/mockups/LandingPage](images/mockups/LandingPage/Landing%20page%20%C2%B7%20desktop.png). Five home page layouts, in the dark palette, are in [images/mockups/HomePages_Dark_Palette](images/mockups/HomePages_Dark_Palette).

## What Tuck believes

- **Rest counts.** Shell days are logged on purpose and count like any other day.
- **Your data stays home.** No account, no analytics on mood entries. Check-ins live in your browser.
- **No streaks.** Tuck counts the days you showed up. Blank days stay blank. Nothing resets to zero.
- **Never red.** Notices are moss, butter or tear. Nothing in the app shames you.

Tuck is a check-in buddy, not a therapist, and gives no medical advice. Need to talk to someone? Call or text 988.

## Run it locally

You need Node.js 20.19 or later.

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

| Command                | What it does                     |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `npm run dev`          | Start the dev server             |
| `npm run build`        | Build for production into `dist` |
| `npm run preview`      | Serve the production build       |
| `npm test`             | Run the tests once (Vitest)      |
| `npm run lint`         | Lint with ESLint                 |
| `npm run format:check` | Check formatting with Prettier   |

The stack is React with Vite, React Router, TanStack Query, Zustand, React Hook Form with Zod, and Tailwind CSS, all in JavaScript.

The app is built on the Tuck the Snail design system: its colors (light and dark), type, spacing, six components (`src/components/`) and the ten mascot SVGs (`public/mascot/`). The home page has a working check-in that saves today's answer in your browser. The calm home (`/calm`, linked from the home page) is a quieter version in the low stimulation palette, ported from `images/Tuck Calm Home/`; both views share the same saved check-in. In the nav, Emotional Check opens the calm home; the Games page (`/games`) holds the shelf of cosmetic unlocks. Every page opens with tuckshell, a working terminal under the nav: type `help` to see its commands (`checkin`, `today`, `log`, `open`, `theme`, `clear`). The rest of pocket@shell (habits, grounding, insights, profile) isn't built yet. For which Tailwind class matches which token, see [docs/design-system/USING-IN-CODE.md](docs/design-system/USING-IN-CODE.md).

